    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d200">

                <pb facs="0364" n="232" sameAs="frus:frus1945Berlinv01/pg232"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">103.9169/7–845: Telegram</note>
                <head>No. 200 <lb/>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chief of the Mission for Economic Affairs in the
                        United Kingdom</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Blaisdell</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Assistant Secretary of State</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Clayton</hi>)<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d200-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">restricted</hi>
                        <lb/>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">us urgent—niact
                        </hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-07-08T16:00:00+02:00">July 8, 1945—4
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>6878. From Blaisdell for Clayton.</p>
                <p>After careful consideration and consultation with Amb
                    Winant, we believe that Soviet Govt
                    should be informed that US intends to raise questions at Berlin Conference
                    concerning organization for production and distribution of German coal on open
                    account as indicated in Dept’s 5415.<anchor
                        n="2" xml:id="d200-f2"/> We feel it would be more effective for Dept to notify Soviet Govt from Washington where
                    agenda for Berlin Conference has been formulated than through Embassies in
                        London.<anchor n="3" xml:id="d200-f3"/>
                </p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
